Local SLM Integration via C# & Microsoft.Extensions.AI
Yesterday, learned the mathematical mechanics of Local Quantization (FP16 down to INT4) and analyzed how GGUF layouts handle system memory split-execution.
Today, I am bringing everything down to production C# code. Instead of coupling an enterprise ecosystem to proprietary cloud wrappers, I am engineering a local, high-throughput intelligence pipeline within .NET using the native, standardized Microsoft.Extensions.AI ecosystem. I will tie a highly compressed local SLM (phi4:mini or llama3) running via an Ollama local backend directly into a C# application, setting up dependency injection, resilience streaming, and structured JSON parsing.

The Big Idea:
Historically, building AI features in .NET meant navigating fragmented, vendor-specific SDKs. If you wanted to switch from Azure OpenAI to a local Ollama instance, you had to swap entire libraries and rewrite your chat orchestration logic.
Microsoft.Extensions.AI solves this by introducing a unified interface layer for generative AI components, built natively into the .NET configuration and dependency injection ecosystem. The star of the show is IChatClient. Whether your model is a 175-billion parameter remote cloud API or a 3-billion parameter quantized local SLM, it implements IChatClient.  
Today, building a local-first engineering pattern: spinning up a local model provider, attaching token stream processing, and implementing automated retry pipelines—all keeping data securely within our local boundary.

