This service configures a localized execution container, using ChatClientBuilder to chain cross-cutting behaviors like caching and telemetry without coupling individual business handlers to specific model variations.

using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Distributed;
using OllamaSharp;

namespace LocalAiEngine;

public class LocalInferenceService
{
    private readonly IChatClient _chatClient;

    public LocalInferenceService(IDistributedCache cache)
    {
        // 1. Establish the base handler pointing to our local engine endpoint
        IChatClient baseClient = new OllamaApiClient(
            new Uri("http://localhost:11434/"), 
            modelName: "phi4:mini"
        );

        // 2. Build our pipeline applying the Decorator Pattern for cross-cutting concerns
        _chatClient = new ChatClientBuilder(baseClient)
            .UseDistributedCaching(cache) // Checks cache before consuming physical memory execution cycles
            .UseFunctionInvocation()      // Enables the local SLM to execute native C# tools if requested
            .Build();
    }

    public async Task ExecuteStreamingPromptAsync(string userPrompt)
    {
        var messages = new List<ChatMessage>
        {
            new(ChatRole.System, "You are a precise, low-latency edge computing assistant. Keep answers factual."),
            new(ChatRole.User, userPrompt)
        };

        Console.Write("AI: ");
        
        // 3. Process the token stream asynchronously to preserve standard system memory allocations
        IAsyncEnumerable<ChatResponseUpdate> tokenStream = 
            _chatClient.GetStreamingResponseAsync(messages);

        await foreach (var token in tokenStream)
        {
            Console.Write(token.Text);
        }
        Console.WriteLine("\n");
    }
}

Key Takeaways:
1. Unified Interfaces Eliminate Vendor Lock-In
The introduction of standard abstractions like IChatClient and IEmbeddingGenerator represents a massive shift for enterprise software design. By decoupling application logic from vendor-specific SDKs, you can change your underlying model provider (e.g., swapping a high-cost cloud endpoint for a local, quantized 4-bit edge model) by simply modifying your Dependency Injection configuration—without rewriting a single line of orchestration code.
2. The Decorator Pattern Powers Clean AI Middleware
Using structural pipeline wrappers like ChatClientBuilder allows you to attach critical production features as modular, cross-cutting concerns. You can cleanly stack infrastructure behaviors such as distributed semantic caching, logging telemetry, performance tracking, and automated function tool execution directly onto your model client without cluttering your core business services.
3. Streaming is Mandatory for Local Heap Optimization
When hosting small language models locally on your own infrastructure, traditional blocking calls can cause severe memory fragmentation and latency. Utilizing IAsyncEnumerable to stream tokens asynchronously back to the client processes text deltas immediately, preventing huge continuous string array allocations within the .NET Garbage Collector Heap (Gen 0).
4. Edge Caching Protects Physical Compute Cores
Unlike cloud APIs where a cache-miss only costs money, a cache-miss on a local server costs raw hardware capacity. Attaching distributed or in-memory caching to your local inference engine is a critical defense mechanism—it ensures that repetitive queries or high-volume background tasks are intercepted at the boundary before they consume valuable CPU/GPU cycles.
